  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 1246 AM MDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nLooking at the rest of this week, southwest flow continues moving \ninto our region, giving us a persistent tap of monsoon moisture \nuntil at least Thursday. PWATS remain well above normal across model \nsolutions, leading to a persistent heavy rain chance across our \nsouthern mountains and plains. Best coverage will be Wednesday Night \ninto Thursday, leading to heightened flash flooding potential, \nparticularly over the burn scars as well as mountainous areas that \nhave received a lot of rain throughout the week. As such, have \nissued a Flood Watch for the San Juans, where QPF has been heaviest \nin the last few days' worth of model runs. Temperatures will remain \nlargely consistent through most of the week, with highs in the 70s \nto low-80s over the plains, and 60s to low-70s in the high valleys.\n\nDrier, warmer conditions will set in across the region, starting on \nFriday and heading into this weekend. Flow aloft will turn more \nwesterly, with downsloping cutting the chance for heavy rainfall, \nthough enough moisture will remain int he area for some isolated \nafternoon convection each day. High temperatures will warm a few \ndegrees, especially over the plains, climbing into the mid-80s by \nthe start of next week. Looking at the broader synoptic pattern, \nlatest models indicate a brief upper ridge lasting through the \nweekend, before an upper trough moves into our region sometime on \nMonday.",
